  <h1>Methodology &mdash; How We Estimate Brain Injury Compensation</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>This page documents how the calculator on the homepage arrives at a recovery range. The estimate is built from published settlement data, neurotrauma severity classification (Glasgow Coma Scale, Rancho Los Amigos Scale, Disability Rating Scale), life-care planning frameworks, and the structured damages categories that govern catastrophic injury litigation. Each component is briefly explained below.</p>

  <h2>Severity classification: GCS, Rancho, and DRS</h2>

  <p>Brain injury severity is the single most important variable in case valuation. Three published frameworks govern how severity is documented and discussed in litigation.</p>

  <h3>Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)</h3>

  <p>The GCS is the universal acute-care severity score, recorded at admission and serially during the first hours and days. Scored 3&ndash;15 across eye opening, verbal response, and motor response. The litigation conventions are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>GCS 13&ndash;15</strong>: mild TBI / concussion.</li>
    <li><strong>GCS 9&ndash;12</strong>: moderate TBI.</li>
    <li><strong>GCS 3&ndash;8</strong>: severe TBI.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A clean lowest-recorded GCS in the chart is the single most useful documentary fact a TBI attorney can have. Where a patient is intubated and sedated, the recorded GCS may be misleadingly low; specialist review of the field GCS and pre-intubation hospital GCS is essential.</p>

  <h3>Rancho Los Amigos Scale</h3>

  <p>The Rancho scale describes cognitive recovery through eight (or in the revised version, ten) levels, from Level I (no response) through Level VIII (purposeful, appropriate). Used heavily in inpatient rehabilitation to track recovery and predict outcome. A patient who plateaus at Rancho IV (confused, agitated) has a substantially different long-term outlook from one who progresses through Rancho VII (automatic, appropriate).</p>

  <h3>Disability Rating Scale (DRS)</h3>

  <p>The DRS combines arousal, cognitive function, dependency on others, and employability into a single score from 0 (no disability) to 29 (extreme vegetative state). DRS is widely used in TBI outcome research and is a useful summary number in damages presentations.</p>

  <h2>Damages framework: five components</h2>

  <p>A TBI case typically claims damages in five categories. Each has its own valuation logic.</p>

  <h3>1. Past medical expenses</h3>

  <p>Documented costs from injury through trial or settlement: emergency response, acute hospitalization (typically $50K&ndash;$500K+ for severe TBI), neurosurgical procedures where applicable, inpatient rehabilitation (typically $50K&ndash;$200K for moderate-to-severe cases), outpatient therapy (cognitive, speech, occupational, physical), neuropsychological evaluation, medications, and equipment. Claimed at billed (or sometimes paid) rates depending on the jurisdiction's collateral source rule.</p>

  <h3>2. Future medical expenses</h3>

  <p>The largest single component for severe TBI. Itemized by a life-care planner (certified life care planner, physiatrist, or rehabilitation specialist), year by year through expected lifespan. Key categories for moderate-to-severe TBI:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Attendant care</strong> &mdash; the largest expense for catastrophic TBI. Severe TBI with cognitive impairment often requires 24-hour supervision or attendant care, costing $150K&ndash;$300K+ per year in most U.S. markets. Vegetative or minimally conscious outcomes may require skilled nursing facility care, which can exceed $400K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Cognitive and behavioral rehabilitation</strong> &mdash; ongoing neuropsychological therapy, cognitive retraining, behavioral programs, and (for some patients) day-program participation. $30K&ndash;$80K/year ongoing.</li>
    <li><strong>Speech and swallowing therapy</strong> &mdash; significant for patients with aphasia, dysarthria, or dysphagia. $15K&ndash;$40K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Medications</strong> &mdash; antiseizure medications, antispasticity drugs, mood stabilizers, sleep aids, and stimulants for cognitive symptoms. $5K&ndash;$25K/year.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipment and home modifications</strong> &mdash; communication devices, mobility equipment, accessible housing, vehicle modifications. $50K&ndash;$200K initial plus recurring replacements.</li>
    <li><strong>Hospitalizations and emergencies</strong> &mdash; TBI patients have elevated rates of seizure-related admissions, behavioral crises, aspiration pneumonia, and orthopedic injury from falls.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The life-care plan projects these costs through expected lifespan (TBI generally reduces lifespan modestly relative to the uninjured population, with greater reductions for catastrophic injury). An economist then reduces them to present value using a discount rate. Future medical care for severe TBI at age 30 with attendant care needs commonly produces present-value totals of $5M&ndash;$12M+.</p>

  <h3>3. Lost earnings (past and future)</h3>

  <p>Calculated as pre-injury earning capacity reduced by post-injury residual earning capacity, projected through expected work-life expectancy. Components:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past lost wages: documented wages lost from injury through trial.</li>
    <li>Future lost earning capacity: pre-injury career trajectory minus residual earning capacity. A vocational expert evaluates what work the patient can realistically perform post-injury given documented cognitive deficits.</li>
    <li>Lost benefits: health insurance, retirement contributions, employer-paid life insurance.</li>
    <li>Household services: value of services the injured person previously performed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>TBI is particularly damaging to cognitive work. A construction laborer with a moderate TBI may regain enough function to return to similar physical work; a software engineer with the same injury may permanently lose the ability to perform complex problem-solving and lose nearly the entire pre-injury income stream. Vocational rehabilitation analysis is critical and case-specific.</p>

  <h3>4. Pain and suffering (non-economic damages)</h3>

  <p>Compensation for the injury itself, ongoing headaches and pain, mood disturbance, loss of identity, loss of life enjoyment, and the impact of cognitive impairment on daily life. Often calculated as a multiplier of economic damages, with typical multipliers ranging from 1.5x to 5x economic damages for TBI. Mild TBI cases often rely heavily on this category because economic damages can be modest while the qualitative life impact is substantial.</p>

  <p>Some states cap non-economic damages in specific contexts (e.g., medical-malpractice non-economic caps). Most states have no caps on non-economic damages in non-medical-malpractice catastrophic injury cases.</p>

  <h3>5. Loss of consortium and family damages</h3>

  <p>Available in most states for the spouse and (in some states) children and parents. TBI creates particularly severe consortium impact because the personality, judgment, and emotional regulation of the injured person often change permanently. Loss of consortium claims add $500K&ndash;$5M+ to a typical catastrophic TBI case.</p>

  <h2>Modifier: jurisdiction</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>California, New York, Pennsylvania, Illinois (Cook County), New Jersey:</strong> well-developed catastrophic injury practice, no general damages caps, plaintiff-friendly juries. Median recoveries often 10&ndash;20% above national average.</li>
    <li><strong>Florida, Massachusetts, Maryland, Connecticut:</strong> standard plaintiff jurisdictions; recoveries near national average.</li>
    <li><strong>Texas, Georgia, and several Southern and Mountain West states:</strong> generally less plaintiff-friendly, with various damage caps or conservative juries. Recoveries often below national average for similar cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Modifier: defendant insurance and assets</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Auto accident with minimum insurance:</strong> a driver with $25K/$50K state-minimum auto insurance can be sued for full damages, but practical recovery is limited to the insurance plus personal assets. Underinsured motorist coverage on the victim's own policy may apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto accident with commercial defendant:</strong> trucking, ride-share, and commercial vehicles typically carry $1M&ndash;$5M policies; recovery up to limits is realistic.</li>
    <li><strong>Premises liability:</strong> commercial premises owners typically carry $1M&ndash;$5M policies plus umbrella coverage.</li>
    <li><strong>Workplace injury with third-party claim:</strong> workers' compensation typically caps the employer claim; a third-party action against an equipment manufacturer or premises owner may add recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical malpractice / birth injury:</strong> hospitals and physicians typically carry $1M&ndash;$3M policies with excess coverage layers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Modifier: age and injury type</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Age under 25 adds ~25% to estimated range (longer life of damages, larger lost-earnings).</li>
    <li>Age 25&ndash;40 adds ~10%.</li>
    <li>Age over 65 reduces ~15% (shorter remaining life expectancy).</li>
    <li>Diffuse axonal injury, anoxic injury, and penetrating injury carry small upward adjustments because they tend to produce more diffuse cognitive impairment and worse functional outcome than focal injuries with similar GCS.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked example: moderate TBI</h2>

  <p>Consider a 35-year-old knowledge worker with a moderate TBI (GCS 11 at admission, focal contusion plus DAI on MRI) from a motor vehicle accident in California, pre-injury income $80K:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past medical expenses (acute hospitalization, inpatient rehab, outpatient cognitive and speech therapy): ~$220K.</li>
    <li>Future medical care (ongoing cognitive rehab, psychiatric medication management, periodic neuropsychological re-evaluation, present value): ~$650K.</li>
    <li>Lost earnings (partial return to work at reduced capacity; present value of differential): ~$1.6M.</li>
    <li>Pain and suffering (2.5x multiplier on economic damages): ~$6.2M.</li>
    <li>Loss of consortium for spouse: ~$500K.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Total nominal damages claim: ~$9.2M. Practical settlement depends on available insurance; typical settlement for this profile in California is $1.5M&ndash;$4M.</p>

  <h2>Worked example: severe TBI</h2>

  <p>Consider a 28-year-old with severe TBI (GCS 6 at admission, diffuse axonal injury, plateau at Rancho VI) from a workplace fall in California, pre-injury income $55K:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Past medical expenses (acute care, neurosurgery, extended inpatient rehab, day program through trial): ~$900K.</li>
    <li>Future medical care (24-hour supervision, ongoing cognitive rehab, equipment, present value): ~$7.5M.</li>
    <li>Lost earnings (unable to return to competitive employment; present value of full lost-earnings stream): ~$2.4M.</li>
    <li>Pain and suffering (3x multiplier on economic damages): ~$32M.</li>
    <li>Loss of consortium for spouse: ~$1.5M.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Total nominal damages claim: ~$44M. Practical settlement constrained by insurance and assets; often settles in the $4M&ndash;$12M range for cases of this profile depending on third-party liability and policy structure.</p>

  <h2>What the calculator does NOT include</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Punitive damages</strong> &mdash; available for gross negligence (drunk driving with priors, intentional safety violations). Can add millions but are case-specific.</li>
    <li><strong>Bad-faith claims against insurance carriers</strong> &mdash; available in some states when an insurer unreasonably refuses to settle within policy limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Specific structured settlement arrangements</strong> &mdash; lump-sum vs. periodic payments materially affect post-settlement economics.</li>
    <li><strong>SSDI, Medicaid, and public benefits</strong> &mdash; available independently of civil litigation; interact with settlement structure for long-term family financial planning (special needs trusts).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Honest about uncertainty</h2>

  <p>TBI case valuations vary widely based on factors a calculator cannot capture: defendant insurance availability, liability disputes, jurisdiction-specific rules, the specific neuropsychological profile, vocational impact, and family circumstances. The calculator's range is a starting reference, not a prediction. An experienced neurotrauma attorney can provide much more specific valuation after reviewing the actual facts and medical record.</p>

